.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd September 29, 2026
.Dt DMESG 8
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm dmesg
.Nd display the kernel message buffer
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl ac
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility prints the kernel message buffer: every message the kernel has
printed, oldest first, as far back as the buffer reaches.
Its size is the
.Va kern.msgbufsize
tunable.
Reading it through
.Nm
leaves it unchanged;
.Xr syslogd 8
reads the same messages once, through
.Pa /dev/klog .
.Pp
A line that carries a
.Sm off
.Ql < Ar priority Ql >
.Sm on
prefix is shown only if its facility is the kernel's, and without the prefix.
If the buffer has wrapped, its first line is incomplete and is left out.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl a
Show the buffer exactly as stored, prefixes and all.
.It Fl c
Empty the buffer after printing it.
Only the superuser may.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sysctl 8 ,
.Xr syslogd 8
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
utility first appeared in
.Bx 3.0 .
This one is OxideBSD's own, written in Rust.
